#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# fourier_transform

#% text
# `fourier_transform(f, t, w)` gives the Fourier transform `1/sqrt(2 pi) integrate(f*exp(im*w*t), [t, -inf, inf])` of `f`.

#% text
# `fourier_transform(f, [t1, t2], [w1, w2])` transforms in several variables, one after the other.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Sums are transformed term by term; a term with an undefined function stays as a `fourier_transform` call.

#% item
# Polynomials, exponentials `exp(im c t)`, `sin` and `cos` give delta functions: `t^k` gives `sqrt(2 pi) (-im)^k` times the k-th derivative of `dirac_delta(w)`.

#% item
# Steps, `sign(t)`, `1/t`, powers and logarithms of `abs(t)`, `sin(a t)/t` and `sech` come from a table; `exp(im c t) g(t)` gives `G(w + c)`.

#% item
# Factors `unit_step(t - a)` and `unit_step(b - t)` become the bounds of the integral; Gaussians `exp(-a t^2 + b t + c)` (with `re(a) > 0`) have a closed form.

#% item
# Even and odd functions go through the cosine and sine transforms, so that the value holds for every real `w` (`exp(-abs(w))`); rational functions without real poles by residues, with `heaviside_theta` of `w` choosing the half plane.

#% item
# Otherwise the integral is left to `integrate`; when it gives no value the call stays unevaluated.

#% item
# `fourier_parameters: [a, b]` gives the transform `sqrt(abs(b)/(2 pi)^(1 - a)) integrate(f*exp(im*b*w*t), [t, -inf, inf])`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A rectangular pulse:

#% cell
fourier_transform(unit_step(t)*unit_step(1 - t), t, w)
#% output
#= (im - im*cos(w) + sin(w))*2^(1/2)/(2*pi^(1/2)*w)

#% text
# A Gaussian:

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-t^2), t, w)
#% output
#= (1/2)*2^(1/2)*exp((-1/4)*w^2)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Distributions:

#% cell
fourier_transform(1, t, w)
#% output
#= dirac_delta(w)*2^(1/2)*pi^(1/2)

#% cell
fourier_transform(sin(a*t), t, w)
#% output
#= -im/2*dirac_delta(a + w)*2^(1/2)*pi^(1/2) + (im/2)*dirac_delta(w - a)*2^(1/2)*pi^(1/2)

#% cell
fourier_transform(heaviside_theta(t), t, w)
#% output
#= (im/2)*2^(1/2)/(pi^(1/2)*w) + (1/2)*dirac_delta(w)*2^(1/2)*pi^(1/2)

#% cell
fourier_transform(t^2, t, w)
#% output
#= -2^(1/2)*pi^(1/2)*dirac_delta''(w)

#% text
# Powers of abs(t):

#% cell
fourier_transform(1/sqrt(abs(t)), t, w)
#% output
#= abs(w)^(-1/2)

#% text
# A band-limited signal:

#% cell
fourier_transform(sin(3*t)/t, t, w)
#% output
#= (1/4)*(sign(3 + w) + sign(3 - w))*2^(1/2)*pi^(1/2)

#% text
# A rational function:

#% cell
fourier_transform(1/(1 + t^2), t, w)
#% output
#= (1/2)*2^(1/2)*exp(-abs(w))*pi^(1/2)

#% text
# A list:

#% cell
fourier_transform([exp(-t^2), exp(-2*t^2)], t, w)
#% output
#= [(1/2)*2^(1/2)*exp((-1/4)*w^2), (1/2)*exp((-1/8)*w^2)]

#% text
# A Gaussian with a symbolic width, and a Lorentzian, with the facts that make them transforms:

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-a*t^2), t, w, assumptions: a > 0)
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)*exp(-w^2/(4*a))/(2*a^(1/2))

#% cell
fourier_transform(1/(t^2 + a^2), t, w, assumptions: a > 0)
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)*exp(-a*abs(w))*pi^(1/2)/(2*a)

#% text
# The hyperbolic secant gives a hyperbolic secant; a damped exponential gives a Lorentzian, and a one-sided one a complex value:

#% cell
fourier_transform(sech(t), t, w)
#% output
#= (1/2)*2^(1/2)*pi^(1/2)*sech((1/2)*pi*w)

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-abs(t)), t, w)
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)/(pi^(1/2)*(1 + w^2))

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-t)*unit_step(t), t, w)
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)/(pi^(1/2)*(2 - 2im*w))

#% text
# A shift in time and a modulation:

#% cell
fourier_transform(dirac_delta(t - 1), t, w)
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)*exp(im*w)/(2*pi^(1/2))

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(3*im*t)*exp(-t^2), t, w)
#% output
#= (1/2)*2^(1/2)*exp((1/4)*(3im + im*w)^2)

#% text
# Distributions of the absolute value and the sign:

#% cell
fourier_transform(sign(t), t, w)
#% output
#= im*2^(1/2)/(pi^(1/2)*w)

#% cell
fourier_transform(abs(t), t, w)
#% output
#= -2^(1/2)/(pi^(1/2)*abs(w)^2)

#% subsection
# Options

#% cell
fourier_transform(dirac_delta(t), t, w, fourier_parameters: [1, -1])
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# The three common conventions: `[0, 1]` (the default) is symmetric, `[1, 1]` has no factor in front, `[0, 2*pi]` is the frequency form:

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-abs(t)), t, w, fourier_parameters: [0, 1])
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)*(pi^-1)^(1/2)/(1 + w^2)

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-abs(t)), t, w, fourier_parameters: [1, 1])
#% output
#= 2/(1 + w^2)

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-abs(t)), t, w, fourier_parameters: [0, 2*pi])
#% output
#= 2/(1 + 4*pi^2*w^2)

#% text
# A number for `w` gives the value there, to the precision of `w`:

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-t^2), t, 2.0)
#% output
#= 0.2601300475114445

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-t^2), t, n(1, 30))
#% output
#= 0.5506953149031837476159810627496478

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The inverse transform gives the function back:

#% cell
inverse_fourier_transform(fourier_transform(exp(-t^2), t, w), w, t)
#% output
#= exp(-t^2)

#% text
# At a number the value is the integral:

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-t^2), t, 2.0) - n_integrate(exp(-t^2)*cos(2*t), [t, -inf, inf])/sqrt(2*pi)
#% output
#= 5.551115123125783e-17

#% text
# The default convention is `fourier_parameters: [0, 1]`:

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-t^2), t, w) - fourier_transform(exp(-t^2), t, w, fourier_parameters: [0, 1])
#% output
#= 0

#% text
# With the default parameters `exp(-t^2/2)` is its own transform:

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-t^2/2), t, w)
#% output
#= exp((-1/2)*w^2)

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# An undefined function stays a call, and the result of a distribution transform may keep `abs(w) sign(w)` unsimplified:

#% cell
fourier_transform(f(t), t, w)
#% output
#= fourier_transform(f(t), t, w)

#% cell
fourier_transform(t*exp(-t^2), t, w)
#% output
#= (im/4)*w*2^(1/2)*exp((-1/4)*w^2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `inverse_fourier_transform`, `fourier_sin_transform`, `fourier_cos_transform`, `laplace_transform`, `integrate`
